[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/ger/20_28mm_s_Pz_B_41_Kursk1943]
2.8/2 cm schwere Panzerbüchse 41 heavy anti-tank rifle

The first patent on a cone-bore rifle and matching round was obtained in 1903 by Professor Karl Puff. A set of trials on it was conducted in 1920s and 1930s by another German engineer, Gerlich, at the German Experimental Institute of firearms in Berlin. The sample 7mm anti-tank Gerlich rifle had a 1700 meters-per-second muzzle velocity. Some time later, the muzzle velocity of Gerlich rifle was successfully increased to 1760 mps. The continued development resulted in the s.Pz.B.41, produced by Mauser-Werke AG, which was adopted for service at the beginning of 1940. (Interestingly, the initial plan was to create a versatile automatic gun with an 18-round magazine.) The rifle was initially designated the Gerät 231 or the Mk.8202. In June and July of 1940, an experimental run of 94 (or other sources say 30) pieces was given to the army for trials. The trials led to some modifications, and from February of 1941, these rifles were being mass produced and delivered into the hands of regular forces.

The S.Pz.B.41 was unusual in design. The rifle breech was of 28mm in diameter; the chase was 20mm in diameter. The rounds were specially designed to crumple and narrow while moving through the barrel. This design allowed increasing muzzle velocity significantly and, accordingly, the piercing performance increased as well, while the dimensions of the rifle remained low. To the present day the classification of this rifle remains controversial. In Germany, it was officially classified as a heavy antitank rifle, while official Soviet papers of the war years classified it as an antitank gun. This fragmentation projectile was of a rather heavy gauge (28mm), and the whole mechanism, was mounted on a wheeled base with a recoil mechanism, which prevented the s.Pz.B.41 from being moved without dismounting it. These details alongside the number of men in the gun-crew suggests that it belongs in the family of large guns. But it could be classified as an antitank rifle because it has no traversing and elevating mechanisms, and the barrel itself could be directly moved and tilted.

There are differing viewpoints on this piece of ordnance amongst the experts. The main advantages of this artillery system are its low dimensions and weight and high piercing performance. The ordnance weighted only 229 kg (and a special modification for landing troops weighed only 139 kg), it was 84 cm high when placed in travel position and a mere 56 cm high in firing position. Should hand-carrying be necessary, it could easily be broken down into 7 parts, without the need of any tools. The piercing performance of the s.Pz.B.41 allowed it to engage medium and even heavy tanks from close range. (There is at least one documented case of the IS-2 rifle piercing the lower front plate of the hull of a heavy tank.) Among its disadvantages are various difficulties in manufacture, weak fragmentation effect once it punches through armour, and the necessity of using scarce tungsten in the production of its armour-piercing projectiles (which eventually led to halting the production of its ordnances), as well as low barrel service life (about 500 shots).

The s.Pz.B.41 was used in various antitank divisions, as well as in antitank detachments of the infantry, the panzergrenadier and tank divisions of the Wehrmacht and SS, and the Luftwaffe airborne divisions. The ordnance was used on every front line until the end of the war. A total of 2,797 s.Pz.B.41 were produced until the series was terminated in September, 1943.
They were mounted on Sd.Kfz.250 armoured personnel carriers (of the Sd.Kfz.250/11 modification), and sometimes mounted on Sd.Kfz.221 armoured cars as well. In addition, the 2.8/2 cm KwK 42 tank gun was based on the s.Pz.B.41. It distinguished itself by featuring a chromium-lined barrel. A total of 24 of these units were produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/ger/37mm_PaK_35_36]
37mm Panzerabwehrkanone 35/36 anti-tank gun
Germany created its first anti-tank gun during the First World War. It was the 37mm Model 18 gun. According to the Treaty of Versailles, Germany was not allowed to have anti-tank artillery, so subsequent work in the area was conducted in secrecy. Work on the new design began as early as 1924, and by 1926 Rheinmetall AG had a prototype ready, a 3.7 cm Model 26 anti-tank gun. The weapon was accepted for service in 1928 as TaK 28, followed by the TaK 29 variant that went into mass production. This gun, despite having wooden wheels with no tires, was used as a base for the PaK 35/36. Interestingly enough, several examples of the TaK 29 were sold to the Soviet Union, where they became the basis for the famous M1937 45mm anti-tank gun. 

The PaK 35/36 was a very advanced weapon for its time, combining good armour penetration, high rate of fire, and low weight. One of the main drawbacks was a HE projectile of limited effectiveness.

PaK 35/36 became the mainstay anti-tank gun of the Wehrmacht between 1935 and 1941, used in separate anti-tank divisions, as well as with anti-tank detachments of infantry, panzergrenadier and panzer divisions. For example, on paper each infantry division was supposed to have 75 guns of this type.

The gun saw action for the first time during the Spanish Civil War, where it proved highly effective against lightly armoured T-26 and BT tanks. Engaging Polish armour in 1939 also proved easy; however during the Battle of France the gun proved less than capable when facing better armoured French and British tanks. Based on the experience, Germany quickly began to replace the gun with a more powerful 50mm PaK 38. However by the invasion of the Soviet Union in June of 1941 the 35/36 remained the most numerous anti-tank gun in active service. 

Engagements with Soviet T-34 and KV tanks quickly proved the gun obsolete. However, despite a common myth, PaK 35/36 was not completely ineffective against a T-34. At short range, a regular armour-piercing projectile could penetrate 40mm of T-34’s side armour or the turret.  The gun was made even more competitive when using APCR projectiles, effective at short range. Additionally, a Stiel.Gr.41 muzzle bomb was developed for the weapon in late 1941, capable of penetrating 180 mm of armour; however accuracy and range of this shot were very limited. By late 1941 PaK 35/36 was being actively replaced by the more powerful PaK 40, but remained in active service until the end of the war as a light infantry gun. A total of 15,000-some PaK 35/36s were produced by 1942.

The gun was also installed on Sd Kfz 251 and Sd Kfz 250 half-tracks (platoon commander’s vehicles), and also on make-shift anti-tank SPGs based on trucks, prime movers, and captured allied vehicles. A 3.7 cm KwK 36 L/45 tank gun was also created based on the PaK 35/36, and used on early Pz.III variants.


[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/ger/50mm_PaK_38]
50mm Panzerabwehrkanone 38 Anti-Tank Gun

Rheinmetall AG began designing the PaK 38 50mm anti-tank gun in 1935. First prototypes of the new weapon were ready by 1936; however the initial trials were less than successful. The weapon needed serious changes, many of which were due to the weapon’s poor initial ballistics. Getting the gun into serial production took years, with production beginning in late 1939, and the first 17 guns accepted for service even later, in July of 1940. The gun was too late to see action in the Battle of France. However, analysis of engagements with French and British armour led to the decision to increase production. By the time Germany invaded Soviet Union in June of 1941 over a thousand guns were available. They played a large role in the initial engagements with Soviet T-34 tanks.

Technically, the PaK 38 was a rather modern design with a split trail carriage and a semiautomatic breech. The barrel had a muzzle brake. A shield of twin armour plates offered reliable protection against small-arms fire for the gun crew.

Overall, the weapon was rather successful. The gun had formidable performance, allowing it to engage light and medium tanks. When using APCR rounds, of which two variants were available, PaK 38 could even engage heavy tanks. The gun was rather lightweight, and could easily be moved manually when needed. At the same time, the weapon was difficult to manufacture, and was not much cheaper than its more powerful successor the PaK 40. Both of these facts spelled its doom. 

Organizationally the PaK 38 was used in separate anti-tank divisions, as well as with anti-tank detachments of infantry, panzergrenadier and panzer divisions. By 1941 relatively few of these guns reached front-line service, but by 1942 the PaK 38 became the most common offensive gun of the Wehrmacht. It kept the honour until late 1943, when it began to be replaced by the PaK 40. A total of 9,568 PaK 38s were produced between 1939 and 1943, and it remained in active service until the end of the war on all fronts. Unlike other German anti-tank guns, the PaK 38 saw little use with self-propelled guns. A 5 cm KwK L/60 tank gun was created based on it, and used on late Pz.III variants.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/ger/75mm_pak_97_38]
German forces captured several thousands of 75mm divisional guns "Shneider" mod.1897 in 1939 in Poland and in 1940 in France. Polish guns were adopted as 7,5 cm Feldkanone 97(p) and french ones as 7,5 cm Feldkanone 231(f). They were almost identical and were used in second-rate divisions. But in the end of 1941 Wermacht high command realized that existing AT ordnance is not enough effective against Soviet T-34 and KV tanks; production of new anti-tank gun Pak 40 has begun, but at first there were too few of them, and German army needed powerful AT ordnance as soon as possible.
In this situation German engineers noticed large number of captured French weaponry. "As is", numerous mod.1897 guns were inadequate for AT role - they had obsolete one-support gun carriage without cushioning (effectively limiting it's horizontal aiming to 6° and towing speed to 10-12km/h) and relatively short barrel (resulting in low muzzle velocity and low penetration achievable by AP round).
The way out of the situaton was found by installing gun barrel with it's support system on carriage of 50mm PaK 38 gun and reducing the weapon kickback by means of powerful muzzle break. HEAT rounds were used for main ammunition, because their armor penetration is not a function of projectile speed during impact.
First such guns arrived to East front during Summer 1942, but they saw most action in 1943 (37800 HEAT rounds for this guns were spent in 1942 and 371600 in 1943). German soldiers had mixed feelings for this gun (it was not very effective and it's kickback was excessive), but it was used until the end of the war. There are many photos showing these guns in 1943-1944, Wermacht still had 122 of Pak 97/38 and F.K.231(f) guns on 1st March 1945 and 14 of them were at front line.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/ger/75mm_PaK_40]
75mm Panzerabwehrkanone 40 Anti-Tank Gun

Rheinmetall AG began work on the PaK 40 75mm anti-tank gun in 1938. Initially the work proceeded slowly. Army brass saw little need for such a powerful anti-tank weapon, believing that the 37mm PaK 35/36 and the 50mm PaK 38 would be more than sufficient. The situation drastically changed when Germany invaded Soviet Union in the summer of 1941, encountering heavily-armoured Soviet KV and T-34 tanks, which could easily withstand multiple hits from existing weapons. Design work on the new anti-tank gun then became a priority, and by November of 1941 a prototype was ready to be tested. The weapon wasn’t quite ready for production however; first mass-production variants were accepted for service in February of 1941. Altogether, only 2,144 PaK 40s were produced in 1942. The lack of heavy anti-tank weapons was temporarily addressed by using captured Soviet and French guns, designated PaK 36(R) and PaK 97/38.

Technically, the PaK 40 was a further redesign of the PaK 38 gun. The weapon had a semi-automatic horizontal breech, which provided for a high rate of fire. Several modifications of the gun were produced, differing in part in wheel types. 

PaK 40 was without a doubt one of the best anti-tank guns in the world. The weapon’s performance was excellent, allowing it to penetrate the armour of virtually any tank; only the frontal armour of the late-war IS-2 and Churchill tanks could withstand a direct hit. A wide variety of shot types further increased the gun’s capabilities; however few effective APCR rounds were produced. The gun was not without its disadvantages, however. It was a highly specialized anti-tank weapon, with a maximum elevation of only 22 degrees; comparable anti-tank guns, such as the Soviet ZIS-3 were more universal. The gun was also inferior to others in its technological design, which made manufacturing costly. The weapon’s heavy weight meant that, after firing, its wheels would get pounded into the ground by the recoil, making it nearly impossible to rotate or move the weapon manually afterwards. 

Organizationally, the PaK 40 was used in separate anti-tank divisions, as well as with anti-tank detachments of infantry, panzergrenadier and panzer divisions. By as late as the summer of 1943 the weapon was still outnumbered by the lighter PaK 38; PaK 40 finally began to dominate the battlefield by late 1943. A total of 23,303 PaK 40s were produced between 1942 and 1945, making it the most mass-produced large-calibre gun of the Wehrmacht. PaK 40 was also installed on a wide variety of self-propelled mounts, e.g. on the Pz.II chassis as the Marder II, on the Pz.38(t) chassis as the Marder III, and on a variety of captured French tanks, among others.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/ger/88mm_pak_43_41]
88mm AT gun Panzerabwehrkanone 43

Development of the  Pak 43/41 was somewhat peculiar, for this gun was, in reality, a major shift in air defence system design. The 8.8 cm Flak 18/36/37 air defence gun distinguished itself as an excellent antitank asset, but its firing position was simply too high off the ground, which proved a serious disadvantage. To resolve this issue, the 8.8 cm Panzerabwehrkanone 43 was developed. It was a redesigned Flak 41 air defence gun, created by the Krupp company, mounted on a lower carriage with four removable wheels. Pak 43 development began in mid-1942, but it proved very time-consuming, and the Rheinmetall company, Krupp’s main competitor, took advantage of the delay. Using the Pak-43 as their starting point, Rheinmetall engineers designed their own variety of the gun, the Pak 43/41. Its primary difference from its competitor was that it used the carriage of a 10.5 cm le.F.H.18 infantry howitzer, equipped with the wheels of a 15 cm S.Fh.18 heavy gun. Manufacture of the Pak 43/41 began in early 1943, and a total of 1,403 such guns were produced. 
Rheinmetall designers had effectively created a hypertrophic variety of the Pak 40. The gun employed a semi-automatic horizontal breech-block, with a two-chamber recoil compensator screwed onto the barrel. The carriage was initially designed for the howitzer, which made it possible to significantly raise the maximum vertical guidance angle. The gun was fitted with an Sfl.ZF.1a optical sight. The design of the apron shield was identical to that of the Pak 40. The gun fired high-explosive, armour-piercing, subcalibre, and hollow charge shells. 
The Pak 43/41 was a very controversial weapon. On the one hand, the gun had advanced armour-piercing capabilities, as its subcalibre shell penetrated an armour plate 193 mm thick at a distance on one kilometre. However, the gun was very heavy and cumbersome – the crew could hardly move it, and only a limited number of tractors (mostly Sd.Kfz.7) could be used to tow it. Both the Pak 43 and the Pak 43/41 were mounted on the Hornisse (Nashorn) self-propelled antitank artillery system. 

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/45mm_at_gun_mod_1938]
45mm 53-K Antitank Gun model 1937

The history of the famous 'forty-five' began in 1930, when the Soviet government signed a supply contract with the German company Rheinmetall. Rheinmetall was to supply the USSR with samples and design documentation of several artillery systems, including a 37mm antitank gun. After trials, the German gun was adopted for service and entered serial production in 1931 as the 37mm antitank gun model 1930 (1-K). Notably, that the same ordnance, only slightly modified, was accepted for service as the 37mm Pak 35/36 anti-tank gun in Germany. 
As early as 1931, work on increasing the ordnance to 45mm began; this would drastically increase the power of its fragmentation and the piercing performance of the gun. In 1932 this new gun, designated the 45mm anti-tank gun model 1932 (19-K), was accepted for service and entered serial production; it differed from the 37mm gun only by the larger calibre and some minor carriage adjustments. The ordnance continued to be upgraded; thus, in 1933 a new semi-automatic inertial breech mechanism was introduced, and in 1937, metal wheels with rubber tires replaced its wooden wheels.
Also in 1937, the Pak 35/36 was delivered to the USSR from Germany. After examination of the prototype, it was decided that the ordnance would be upgraded to the "forty-five" carriage, in conformance with the German gun. In 1938 the upgraded gun was tested, accepted for service and entered into serial production.

By 1941, the 53-K was quite a modern ordnance. Its ballistics were quite powerful, which allowed it to engage most enemy tanks and SP guns; in addition, fragmentation of the gun's projectile was quite effective against infantry and MG nests. The ordnance was light and compact; it could be hand-carried and easily concealed. But it should also be stated that in 1941 the Soviet armour-piercing projectiles were of low quality, and the ordnance often failed to meet its full potential; but in 1942, after the new type of advanced projectiles were designed, which were encased in pointed solid ‘English’ shells, this problem was solved. But at the same time, the armour plating of German tanks became heavier as well, which required a yet further gun upgrade – and at the end of 1942 the 45mm anti-tank gun model 1942 (M-42) was accepted for service.

The 45mm gun was considered to be the battalion ordnance until July of 1941, and after December of 1942 each battalion included an anti-tank platoon with two guns. Each regiment would include an anti-tank battery with six 'forty-fives'. In addition, separate anti-tank artillery regiments (ATARs) and divisions (ATADs) were equipped with multiple 45mm guns; as a rule an ATAR had twenty such ordnances. The 1937 model of the 45mm anti-tank gun was widely used throughout the war, even though its piercing performance became insufficient in penetrating the new German tank armour produced from the beginning of 1943 (though the use of APCR projectiles alleviated this problem to some extent). The serial production of that gun was terminated only at the beginning of 1944, after a total of fifty thousand guns had been produced. The 45mm 20-K tank guns, similar in design and identical in ballistics, were installed on many Soviet tanks and armoured cars, including the T-26, the BT-7, the T-35, the T-50, the T-70, and the BA-10 in particular.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/45mm_at_gun_mod_1942]
45mm anti-tank gun model 1942 (M-42)
By the very end of 1941, work on modernizing the 45mm anti-tank gun model 1937 (53-K) had begun. It was crucial to increase its armour penetration abilities, as German troops began using tanks and SP guns with 50-60mm front plates, against which the standard 'forty-five' was inefficient. Three design bureaus – Factory No. 8 (ordnance 101-K), Factory No. 172 (ordnance M-6) and OKB-172 (ordnance M-42) – simultaneously began working on a new ordnance design. The M-6 and M-42 were the more promising samples, and both successfully passed the trials, but it was M-42 which was accepted for service, as it was more suited to serial production (whereas the M-6, unlike the M-42, was an entirely new ordnance, and not a development of the 53-K). It is remarkable that OKB-172, where the gun was designed, was a special kind of bureau of the Stalinist period, a so-called sharaga, whose workers were imprisoned engineers. Serial production of the gun began in January, 1943.

The main difference between the M-42 and the 53-K was an extended barrel (68.6 verses 46 calibre), which increased the muzzle velocity of shells and their piercing performance. There were several more minor differences – a thicker shield, the usage of mechanical semi-automatic instead of inertial-mechanical on some ordnances, and so on. The new ordnance was well received, as it had good armour penetration capacity (almost all German tanks and SP guns could be hit in the hull side, and even on the front side if a composite shell were used), the gun was light and compact, which facilitated its transport and camouflage.

M-42 was used as a battalion ordnance – and indeed, each battalion since December 1942 included an anti-tank platoon with two guns (which was raised to four guns in December of 1944). Each regiment included an anti-tank battery with six ‘forty-fives’. In addition, separate anti-tank artillery regiments (ATARs) and divisions (ATADs) were equipped with many M-42 guns, especially in 1943; as a rule an ATAR had twenty such ordnances.  M-42s were actively used from 1943 through the end of the war. Its first serious field application was the Battle of Kursk, though among the other 45mm guns, the M-42 were relatively scarce (numbering no more than 30%). Serial production of the guns was terminated only in 1946, after a total of 10,983 M-42 guns had been produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/57mm_ZIS_2]
57mm Anti-Tank Gun M1943 (ZIS-2)

By the spring of 1940, Soviet military command received intelligence on the heavily armored tanks being built in Germany. Although these reports were not entirely accurate, they nevertheless precipitated a flurry of interest in the development of powerful anti-tank guns. One design bureau entrusted with this development, supervised by V. G. Grabin, had already managed to create a prototype by October 1940. The drawbacks were then addressed, and the new gun was adopted by the army in March of 1941.
At that time, the ZIS-2 was an innovative artillery piece, with outstanding performance. Its key feature was a long 73-caliber barrel, which provided very high muzzle velocity and hence excellent armor penetration. However, the barrel was quite complex, making it difficult to produce, so the gun entered mass production only in the second half of 1941. Within the year, a total of 371 pieces had been manufactured, 101 of which were mounted on ZIS-30 self-propelled anti-tank guns. Late in 1941, production of the ZIS-2 was halted in favor of a more universal-purpose and easily-manufacturable gun - after it became known that the enemy did not possess those heavily armored tanks which the ZIS-2 had initially been created to answer.
But in the spring of 1943, Soviet military command analyzed gunning tests involving captured Tiger tanks, and found that their existing anti-tank capabilities were insufficient. Production of the ZIS-2 resumed, as a way to answer this new threat, and a number of changes were introduced into the gun design.
The newly reconfigured ZIS-2 was adopted by the army in the summer of 1943. These guns were too late to join the Battle of Kursk, as no forces on the Voronezh Front were yet in possession of the new ZIS-2, although the forces of the Central Front already had four tank-destroyer artillery regiments featuring these guns. As the number of ZIS-2 pieces grew, they were actively used throughout the remainder of the war. However, the 45mm and 76mm guns remained the primary Soviet anti-tank guns. Still, the ZIS-2 had a long service history with the Soviet Army, and was exported during the postwar years. In some countries these guns are still in service. ZIS-2 guns were also shipped to the North Korean forces and used in the Korean War.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/76mm_obr_1927_Kursk1943]
76mm Regimental Gun model 1927

In the 1920s, the artillery armament of the Red Army consisted of ordnances of the Imperial Army of Russia, which was, on the whole, successful in meeting its purposes. However there were some problems with the regimental ordnances, and the army lacked a light gun with low ballistics, intended for use in battle formations. The ordnance in service was the 76mm gun model 1902, but it was too heavy its ballistics were too powerful, so it was decided that a new regimental gun must be designed. This new gun would not, however, be created from scratch. It was to be simply a fundamental modernization of the 76mm short gun model 1913 (which was, in turn, based on the original gun model 1909). This modernization, initiated by Sokolov, meant mounting a longer gun chamber for a more powerful shell; and the carriage was also changed significantly (in particular, cushioning was introduced). Serial production of the ordnance, the first mass-produced ordnance designed by Soviet engineers, began in 1928.

Technically it was a short-length gun with a screw breechblock, fixed breeching and a box trail carriage. The gun had its own ammunition, which could also be fired from divisional ordnances. The ordnance was simple and reliable, and it had good shooting range, but it had several disadvantages: its maximum elevation angle was only 24.5 degrees, which, coupled with fixed breeching, did not allow firing overhead; the very low training angle hindered its effectiveness against tanks and armoured cars; and the outdated screw breechblock significantly decreased its rate of fire.
There were two main ordnance modifications, which differed from each other by the type of wheels used (wooden or metal with rubber tires).

The 76mm regimental gun model 1927 was actively used in all pre-war military conflicts in which the USSR participated; they were used in the Great Patriotic War (World War II on the territory of the Soviet Union) from the first day of battle through the end of the war. Each rifle regiment had a battery of four 76mm guns, which proved rather effective against enemy infantry and firing-points. However, until hollow charge projectiles were accepted for service in 1943, they were almost useless against tanks because the low muzzle velocity and correspondingly low piercing ability. By the end of 1943, the production of model 1927 was terminated, as new regimental guns model 1943 were being accepted into service. But in the relatively brief period between 1941-1943, 13,282 pieces were produced. In the 1930s, the gun was mounted on SP guns SU-12, which used truck chassis. The 76mm tank gun KT, which was mounted on BT-7A, T-28 and T-35 tanks, was based on that gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/76_2mm_regimental_gun_1943]
76mm Regimental Gun M1943 (OB-25)

In the late 1930s, Soviet military command ordered a new regimental gun to replace the obsolete M1927. Its drawbacks – especially its heavy weight – had by that time become obvious. Several prototypes were designed in 1938-40, but none were actually adopted. Work on the regimental gun resumed in 1942, but this time it was focused on improving muzzle velocity and armor penetration, but this development was halted once more, after the adoption of shaped charge projectiles. The key requirements had changed once more, and now the new regimental gun would be designed for light weight and a large training angle.
In February of 1943, the OKB-172 factory, which utilized the labor of convicted engineers, developed a new regimental gun which combined a new barrel of moderate ballistic qualities with the carriage of the 45mm anti-tank gun M1942. Field tests conducted in June 1943 exposed a number of drawbacks. Once those were addressed, the gun once more entered operational tests and was adopted for service on September 4, 1943. Mass production was launched late in 1943 and continued until early 1946, over which time a total of 5122 pieces were produced.
The OB-25 was a classic mobilization gun, and although it did not boast outstanding specifications, it was cheap and easy to produce. Its advantages included good mobility and large training angle, and its drawbacks were low muzzle velocity and its inability to fire over head. The gun had only a few types of ammunition, essentially high-explosive and shaped charge shells, which were incompatible with other artillery pieces. It is interesting that German engineers used the same approach to create their IG 37 infantry support gun, which they mounted on the carriages of captured Soviet 45mm anti-tank pieces. However, the German gun’s barrel was quite different, as it featured a muzzle brake, semi-fixed ammunition, and a semi-automatic wedge breechblock.
The 76mm M1943 regimental guns were actively used against the Germans in for the remainder of the war, but their postwar history was brief, and by the 1950s they had been gradually withdrawn. A number of these guns were exported to North Korea, whose forces used them in the Korean War.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/76_2mm_ZIS_3]
76mm Divisional Gun M1942 (ZIS-3)

In the summer of 1941, the design bureau of Factory No. 92, under the supervision of V. G. Grabin, was tasked with improving the 76mm divisional gun M1939 (USV), with the goal of reducing its production cost and increasing manufacturability. Realizing that the design of the USV could not be greatly improved, Grabin began work on a new divisional gun which would combine an upgraded USV barrel assembly with a muzzle break, with the carriage of the 57mm ZIS-2 anti-tank gun. The new artillery piece was quickly constructed, and by the fall of 1941 several new guns entered operational testing. Based on the test results, the new gun was adopted and entered mass production in February 1942, gradually replacing the older gun, the production of which finally ended in late 1942.
The ZIS-3 was a classic mobilization gun, well-suited for mass production, and was rightfully considered one of the best Soviet artillery pieces. Its key advantages included relatively compact dimensions and light weight, reliability, ease of use and production, and low production cost. The drawbacks included a muzzle brake which demasked the gun during firing, and low firing elevation, especially in the 1942-43 models, which limited the gun's efficiency in sheltered fire positions. Better armored German tanks and SPGs, which began to appear on the battlefield in 1943, exposed the insufficient armor penetration of ZIS-3. It was only at very short distances and at normal angles that the gun could penetrate the 80mm the frontal plates of the newer German Pz IV and StuG III, and the Tiger side armor, although the use of sub-caliber rounds did improve matters.
The ZIS-3 saw frequent use from 1942 through the end of the war, over which time it became one of the most widespread Soviet artillery pieces. These guns were used by artillery regiments of rifle divisions, as well as the Artillery units of the Supreme High Command Reserve. In addition, the ZIS-3 was also heavily used by tank-destroyer artillery regiments. Several variants of the gun were made throughout the war. These differed by the maximum on-board elevation (27 and 37 degrees), and in various other respects. One variant designed for tank-destroyer artillery regiments was of lighter build (some pieces didn’t even have gun shields), weighing a scant 950-980kg. These had small firing elevation, making them more of an anti-tank gun than a divisional piece. ZIS-3 production, over the course of 1942-1945, totaled 48,002 guns. The variant designated the ZIS-3Sh was designed for the SU-76, the Soviet Union’s most widespread SPG.
During the postwar years, the ZIS-3 enjoyed a long service history with the Soviet Army, and was also used as a regimental gun. The pieces shipped to the North Korean forces were actively used during the Korean War, where they established a generally-favorable reputation. However, these guns were not well-suited for mountain warfare because of their insufficient firing elevation.
During the postwar years, ZIS-3 guns were exported to a number of countries, where some are still in service today.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/rus/100mm_BS_3]
100mm Field Gun M1944 (BS-3)

By the spring of 1943, Soviet command had analyzed the performance of their own guns against a German Tiger tank which had been captured near Leningrad, and concluded that they must improve the anti-tank capabilities of the Soviet Army. The best and most direct approach was the production of a new and powerful anti-tank gun. This task was given to two design bureaus supervised by V. G. Grabin and F. F. Petrov. The former worked on three guns based on the same carriage: a 100mm S-3 gun with the ballistic capabilities of the B-34 naval gun, a powerful long-barreled 85mm S-3-1, and a 122mm C-4 gun with the capabilities of the A-19 corps gun. The second design bureau focused on a 122mm D-2 gun, which also had the ballistic capability of the A-19.
Designs for the S-3 had been drawn up by June 4, 1943, and on September 14, 1943, a prototype entered field tests -- tests which exposed two drawbacks: an unreliable muzzle break and an overly strong recoil. The prototype had to be sent back for improvements. At the same time, the S-4 and D-2 guns also entered testing. The S-3 gun entered the second round of tests on April 15, 1944, and managed to pass all trials. On May 7, 1944, the gun was adopted for combat use, and became known as the BS-3 M1944 100mm field gun. The first 50 guns had been completed by May 1944, but the BS-3 only reached the front lines at end of the year, due to problems with the manufacture of its armor-piercing shells.
The BS-3 was one of the most powerful anti-tank guns of the World War II, second only to the German 128mm Pak 44, whose semi-fixed ammo and was three times as heavy. Capable of dealing with most of the armored vehicles of the German forces, the BS-3 gun enjoyed active use until 1945.
Mass production of these guns continued into 1951, over which time a total of 3816 pieces were manufactured. Before the 1960s, the BS-3 could inflict significant damage on enemy tanks with its regular armor-piercing caliber shells. Later, its ammunition range was expanded with fin-stabilized sub-caliber and high-explosive shells, as well as the Bastion guided projectile, which was added in the 1980s.
BS-3 guns were not used in the Korean War, but during the postwar years, these guns were exported to a number of countries, and some are still in service today. According to some sources, BS-3 units can still be found in storage at Russian Army depots.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/usa/57mm_AT_gun_M1]
57mm Gun M1

At the beginning of World War II, the 37mm Gun M1 was the primary anti-tank piece of the US Army. One of the best in its class, it met all requirements set down by US military command. However, the British had already adopted well-armored tanks, and they expected the enemy to do the same. Therefore, in 1940 they also adopted the Ordnance QF 6-pounder 7 cwt gun. However, due to problems with mass production, the defeat at Dunkirk, and the loss of most of their anti-tank pieces, it was decided that this gun be replaced with the 40mm artillery piece, which was easier to manufacture. At the same time, the British began negotiations with the United States about lend-leasing mass production of their 6-pounders to the US.
The US received two working samples, along with blueprints, and modernized the design to meet local manufacture requirements. The new gun entered mass production early in 1942, by which time the US Army had shown their own interest in the artillery piece, which it designated the M1.
In terms of design, the M1 was nearly identical to the 6-pounder. Both had the same ballistic capabilities, and used the same ammunition. It is therefore interesting that the US launched mass production of the 50-caliber variant at once, while the British began with the 43-caliber gun and only later moved to the 50-caliber variant. Unlike the US counterpart, the British gun had muzzle break.
Until 1943, the US military had not fully committed to the M1; their forces in North Africa were only armed with 37mm guns. However, after the Tunisian Campaign exposed the limitations of their 37mm guns against well-armored German tanks, the necessity of the M1 was acknowledged, and the gun entered active service. By the time of Normandy landings, it had become the primary anti-tank gun of the US, and remained so for the remainder the war. A total of 15637 M1 guns were manufactured between 1942 and 1945.
By the onset of the Korean War, the US Army had already replaced their M1 guns with recoilless guns. However, the South Korean forces still had many of the older guns in their arsenal, both in the field and in their weapons depots. Given the deficit of anti-tank capabilities, which they suffered during the first months of the war, the South Koreans naturally had to mobilize everything they had. However, combat use showed that the M1 was not sufficient against the frontal armor of T-34-85, in part because there were no sub-caliber shells available.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/usa/76_2mm_M5]
Late in 1940 the Ordnance Corps started a project - an anti-tank gun based on the 3 inch anti-aircraft gun T9. The barrel of the T9 was combined with breech, recoil system and carriage, all adapted from the 105 mm howitzer M2. The pilot of the weapon, named 3 inch Gun T10, was ready by September 1941. Although the subsequent testing revealed minor problems, it was clear that the gun, eventually standardized as M5 on carriage M1, presents major performance improvement over existing designs.

Production began in December 1942. In November 1943 a slightly modified carriage was standardized as M6. In this carriage a flat shield borrowed from the 105 mm howitzer was replaced by a new sloped one. A total of 2500 M5 anti-tank guns were produced till 1944.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/usa/75mm_M20]
75mm Recoilless Rifle M20

The US military was greatly concerned with the anti-tank capability of their infantry, demanding both high mobility and efficiency. In contrast to conventional artillery pieces, the far more maneuverable bazookas, which were adopted in 1942, had a limited accuracy range and poor hit-density. The recoilless rifle, which combined the range and accuracy of conventional artillery pieces with the light weight of bazookas, was the compromise solution that the US military was looking for.
The development of the 57mm and 75mm recoilless guns was launched in 1943, and the first prototypes entered field tests in 1944. The 75mm gun, designated the M20, entered mass production in March of 1945.
The idea behind M20 was the ejection of propellant gases rearward, through special breech holes, to compensate for recoil. This enabled a reduction in barrel weight, and the cumbersome gun carriage and recoil absorbers could be discarded. This 75mm gun could be mounted on the light tripod designed for the M1917А1 machine gun, and could easily be dismantled and carried by the squad. The even lighter 57mm M18 recoilless rifle allowed firing from the shoulder like a bazooka. At the same time, the rifled bore provided much higher accuracy and longer range than rocket launchers. The specialized ammo cartridges of these guns were easily recognizable by the multiple holes for ejecting propellant gases.
M20 ammunition included shaped charge, high-explosive, and smoke rounds. This enabled efficient use of the gun against a variety of targets: tanks, infantry, light fieldworks, and machine gun nests and the like.
One drawback of this design was the large breech blast area. Naturally, no personnel or flammable materials could occupy a large space behind the gun. For the same reason, it was difficult to use recoilless guns inside buildings, or from within dense vegetation or similar environments. In addition, the ejected propellants kicked up clouds of dust, which compromised the gun's position.
The M20 did not see much use in World War II, but it was used very actively in the Korean War as an anti-armor and anti-personnel gun.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/uk/40mm_2pdr_AT_Gun_Mk_II]
40mm Ordnance Q.F. 2-pounder gun Mark IX on Carriage Mark II

British pre-war 2-pounder anti-tank guns were based on a 40mm tank gun, designed to replace the obsolescent 3-pdr Hotchkiss gun of WWI provenance. The new tank gun was quite good for its time, and a decision was made to create an anti-tank gun version, thus simplifying manufacture and logistics. A new tank gun prototype was ready by 1934, and was accepted for service in January of 1935. Work immediately began on an anti-tank carriage variant, with proposals being put forward by both Woolwich Arsenal and Vickers. Field trials completed by July 1936, with both competing designs being awarded contracts, as their performance was nearly identical, and manufacturing realities at both factories meant that selecting a single variant would not be cost-effective. Later on, mainly the Woolwich Arsenal variant was produced due to lower cost. Serial production began in late 1936.

2-pdr anti-tank guns were of rather original design, drastically different from other comparable light anti-tank guns of the period. The carriage was a three-leg platform; this design element made it possible to rotate the weapon 360 degrees with legs unfolded. This however made the weapon much larger and heavier than comparable guns (it weighed 1,994 lbs (814 kg), when a Soviet 45mm M1937 gun weighed only 1,234 lbs (560 kg)). It also increased the amount of time to position the weapon for combat. Two main carriage variants existed, Mark I from Vickers, and Mark II from Woolwich, differing in recoil sleigh assembly and wheel construction. In combat position, the Mk.I’s wheels were lifted up, and Mk.II’s were removed. The 2-pdr’s ammunition included a basic AP round and a pre-war APHE shot. By 1943 a more advanced APCBC shot went into production. Initially no HE rounds were provided altogether, which made the piece of limited use when facing infantry and other soft targets. HE rounds became available only in late 1942, as the 2-pdr was becoming hopelessly obsolete. While in early war the 2-pdr could successfully engage German armor, the entry of heavy Tiger tanks and the armor increase to 80mm on later Pz IV and StuG III variants meant that the gun quickly became ineffective. An interesting interim solution was the so-called Littlejohn adapter, a reducing bore attachment for the business end of the barrel, which when firing the special APCNR round greatly increased muzzle velocity, thus improving armor penetration. These adaptors went into production in January of 1943, but saw limited use, mainly used on Daimler Armored Cars. The 2-pdr’s production was stopped in 1943, being replaced by the more powerful 6- and 17-pounders.

2-pdr anti-tank guns were actively used in 1940 in France, in North Africa in 1941-1943, and in South-East Asia. In addition to UK, the gun was also used by Belgium and Australia. A small batch was also sent to USSR. A total of 11,038 2-pdr anti-tank guns were produced between 1936 and 1943.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/uk/57mm_6pdr_AT_Gun_Mk_II_afrika]
57mm Ordnance Q.F. 6-pounder 7-cwt. gun Mark II Anti-Tank Gun

By 1938, the British military began to realize that the 2-pdr anti-tank gun then in service was inadequate for modern armored warfare. The realization was based, in part, on internal developments, such as the development of the Matilda tank with 60mm armor in 1937. Woolwich Arsenal then began work on a new weapon, choosing the caliber of 57mm for it. 57mm weapons were already used by the Royal Navy, and Woolwich had manufacturing equipment in place. The weapon was designed with the potential of being used as a tank main gun; however, a towed artillery piece was the main priority. By January of 1940 the weapon finished the final round of tests, and it was accepted for service in April of 1940, both in a tank-gun and towed piece variant, even though the carriage for the latter was still incomplete.

The disastrous Battle of France, and the subsequent Dunkirk Evacuation with loss of tremendous amount of heavy equipment led to an increase in demand for new anti-tank guns. Due to assembly lines still set to manufacture the obsolete 2-pdr guns, it was decided that a delay in switching production to the 6-pdr, and in adopting and retraining gun crews was unacceptable. The 6-pdr project was therefore put on the backburner, and a large run of 2-pdr guns was produced instead; interestingly enough, the situation parallels the decision in the Soviet Union to freeze production of their 57mm anti-tank gun in 1941. With the 6-pdr, a decision was made to later concentrate on the tank gun variant, as opposed to the anti-tank artillery piece.

As originally designed, the weapon’s barrel length was 50 calibers; however that made the weapon difficult to install in existing tank turrets. The simplest solution was to redesign the gun, shortening the barrel to 43 calibers. This shortened variant went into production in June 1941 as a tank gun.

After Germany invaded the Soviet Union, it became clear that an invasion of the British Isles would be unlikely for some time; and British forces fighting in North Africa urgently needed a more powerful anti-tank weapon. Considering these conditions, a decision was made to green light the anti-tank variant of the 6-pdr. To unify it with the tank variant, the barrel was also shortened to 43 calibers. Serial production began in November of 1941.

Technically, the gun was a light anti-tank artillery piece of modern construction with recoil sleigh assembly on a split trail carriage. The shortened barrel affected performance, which however remained acceptable; compared to the German PaK 38, the British gun had lower muzzle velocity, but better armor penetration on a direct hit with an AP shell. While it was inferior to the Soviet 57mm ZIS-2, the Russian gun went into service only in the summer of 1943. 

The main initial shortcoming of the 6-pdr was its limited ammunition types. Until 1943 only a basic AP round was available, which had limited post-penetration effectiveness, and was ineffective against sloped or reinforced armor. Improved APC and APCBC rounds became available in early 1943, APCR shot in October of 1943, and a highly effective APDS sabot round in March of 1944. HE shells, were produced since 1942 initially in very limited quantities.

Altogether, by early 1942 standards, the 6-pdr Mk.II was a very successful design, capable of all intended tasks. However the situation changed by 1943 with the introduction of heavy Tiger tanks, and armor increase to 80mm on later Pz IV and StuG III variants. The 6-pdr was of limited effectiveness against such armor, only penetrating it when impacting at shallow angles at close range. To remedy the problem, the Mk.IV variant returned to the initial 50-caliber barrel length.

57mm QF 6-pdr Mk.II saw a lot of action in North Africa, starting with the Battle of Gazala in May of 1942, and subsequently in Italy. The Mk.II was almost phased out by the time the Allies landed in France, being replaced by the 6-pdr Mk.IV and the new 17-pdr guns. The gun was also produced in the US, in its 50-caliber barrel variant. 400 QF 6-pdr Mk.IIs were also sent to the USSR via the lend-lease program.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/uk/57mm_6pdr_AT_Gun_Mk_IV] 
57mm Ordnance QF 6-pounder 7cwt Mark IV

1939 saw the first prototype of the weapon designed to replace the QF 2-pounder Gun. In June of the following year, the army placed an order for 400 pieces, designated the "6 pounder Gun." However, because a large number of QF 2-pounder guns had been lost during the evacuation of British troops from France, there were more urgent calls to increase production of the older gun, and as a result, mass production of the 6-pounder was delayed until November of 1941.

The 6-pounder Gun Mk.II was the model’s first high-volume modification. By the time the Allies landed in Normandy, the 6-pounder Gun Mk.IV had become the most common variant. Its barrel was a full 16 inches longer, and capped with a muzzle brake. Beyond Great Britain, 6-pounders were produced in Canada, and were adopted by the U.S. as their 57-mm Anti-Tank Gun, renamed the M1. The American counterpart was almost identical to the original.

In terms of design, the 6-pounder was a larger version of the 2-pounder, and mounted on a more conventional carriage. Like its predecessor, the 6-pounder featured a vertical breech-block and a hydraulic recoil system. A shoulder support was mounted to the left of the breech end, and a telescopic sight was used for guidance. The gun shield consisted of two sections, upper and lower. The upper section was mounted to the tipping part, while the lower was attached to the carriage. The gun could fire armour-piercing, armour-piercing tracer, and subcalibre shells. High-explosive rounds were also available, but were rarely used due to their low efficiency.

By June 1944, most 6-pounder guns had been replaced by the more powerful 17-pounders. However, the former still saw wide use, and boasted certain advantages over the latter. A 6-pounder gun was half the weight of a 17-pounder, and it had a lower profile. A standard armour-piercing shell fired from a 6-pounder Gun Mk.IV could penetrate a sheet of armour 73mm thick from 1000 meters away – and a subcalibre shell could penetrate up to 146mm armour plate from the same distance.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/uk/76mm_17_25_Pdr_Pheasant]
Although it had not yet entered service, the British predicted that the QF 6-pounder would soon be inadequate given the increasing armour of German tanks. In late 1940 a design effort was started to create a replacement, and the effort was largely complete by the end of 1941. A prototype production line was set up that spring, and with the appearance of Tiger I tanks in North Africa, the first 100 prototype 17-pdr anti-tank guns were quickly sent off to help counter this new threat. So great was the rush that they were sent before proper carriages had been developed, and the guns had to be mounted in the carriages of 25-pounder gun-howitzers. These early weapons were known as 17/25-pounders and given the codename Pheasant. They first saw action in February 1943.

Fully developed 17-pdrs were placed into production in 1943 and were first used during the Italian Campaign. 

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/fr/75mm_puteaux_mle_1897_26]
Canon de Campagne Mle.1897, designed in the second half of the 1890s, became a milestone in artillery history. It was the world’s first gun with a recoil mechanism. Military units got first samples of this gun in 1898, and by the beginning of World War I, Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 became the main field gun of the French army. The gun is often called Puteaux Mle.1897, though it is not quite correct, as it was manufactured at several plants, and it might as well be called Schneider Mle.1897. Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 remained in service for a long time, as French and Polish troops still used it in World War II. In the Polish army, the gun was designated 75-mm Armata wz.1897.
At the time of development, Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 was distinguished for its progressive design. In addition to the recoil system, the gun was the first to use a piston eccentric screw breech-block. Thanks to this innovation, the gun had a rate of fire that was high enough even to World War II standards. A high apron shield ensured protection from fragments, and had an opening for the gun sight in its left section. The carriage had wooden wheels. In 1933, some of the guns were upgraded: their wooden wheels were replaced with steel wheels with pneumatic tires, and the apron shield was lowered. A collimating sight was used to control fire. When the Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 was originally developed, tanks did not yet exist. Nevertheless, the gun performed well in its new role as an antitank weapon. An armor-piercing cartridge, designed specially for this gun, was capable of penetrating a plate 90 mm thick at a distance of 100 m. 
In the beginning of World War II, Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 and 75-mm Armata wz.1897 were the main field guns of the French and Polish armies respectively. Regardless of their respectable age, the guns performed well as a means of combating enemy infantry and tanks. Germans transformed some of the captured weapons into 7.5 cm Pak 97/38 antitank guns.

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/pol/37mm_bofors_wz_36]
In 1932 Swedish company Bofors designed a 37-mm antitank gun, originally designated M/34. Just like many other types of armament designed by Bofors, the gun was export-oriented. In 1939, the Polish government bought the license to manufacture M/34 guns, which were designated 37-mm Armata Przeciwpancerna wz.36. Also a tank gun was derived from this gun in order to equip, in particular, Polish 7TP tanks. By September 1939, the Polish army had procured about 1,200 37-mm Armata Przeciwpancerna wz.36.
Among the undeniable advantages of this gun is its low weight in firing position, amounting only to 380 kg. Thanks to the use of wheels with pneumatic tires, the maximum speed of transporting the gun on the highway was as high as 50 km/h, which was a very good performance for that time. The gun also had a high rate of fire amounting to 10-25 rpm. The gun employed a wedge semi-automatic vertical breech-block. In order to reduce the recoil, the barrel was equipped with a recoil compensator of original design. Fire control was possible through a collimating sight. It fired armor-piercing and high-explosive shells. 
As of the beginning of the war, 37-mm Armata Przeciwpancerna wz.36 was a good antitank gun, capable of piercing the armor of nearly all types of enemy armored vehicles. An armor-piercing shell penetrated a plate 25 mm thick positioned at an angle of 30 degrees at a distance of 500 m. By 1941, the gun became outdated and could only effectively combat light armored targets. 

[EncDescription_data/units/artillery/pol/75mm_field_gun_wz_1897]
Canon de Campagne Mle.1897, designed in the second half of the 1890s, became a milestone in artillery history. It was the world’s first gun with a recoil mechanism. Military units got first samples of this gun in 1898, and by the beginning of World War I, Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 became the main field gun of the French army. The gun is often called Puteaux Mle.1897, though it is not quite correct, as it was manufactured at several plants, and it might as well be called Schneider Mle.1897. Canon de Campagne Mle.1897 remained in service for a long time, as French and Polish troops still used it in World War II. In the Polish army, the gun was designated 75-mm Armata wz.1897.
